open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts src/configuration/errors/default.json src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
First job of the chapter. No new keys from here to the end of the dojo —
everything below is something you already have.

    ORD-771  Cancelling an order that is already cancelled refunds it a
             second time. Support have had four of these this week.
             Reject the second cancel with a 409.

  1. src/domain/order/order.provider.ts — in `cancel`, directly after the
     SHIPPED guard and separated from it by one blank line, reject an
     order that is already cancelled with `ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED`.
     Same shape as the guard above it.

  2. src/configuration/errors/default.json — register the code directly
     under `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`. A 409, not reported to Sentry: this
     is a client mistake, not our bug.

  3. src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts — open so you can see that the
     mutation needs no change. Touch it and the check fails.

The new guard is the old guard with two words different. Type it twice
and you are doing the job the slow way.

@keys
| `4Y` | yank four lines |
| `p` | put them back below |
| `ci'` `ci"` | change inside the quotes |
| `:bn` `:bp` | walk the buffers |
| `:wqa` | write everything and leave |

@hints
Nothing in this ticket is new code. Both files already contain a line
that is nearly the line you want.
--
In the provider, put the cursor on the `if (order.status === 'SHIPPED')`
line and yank four lines — the guard and the blank line under it. Move
down to the blank line and put. Now you have two guards and the spacing
is already right.
--
Edit the copy, not the original. `f'` then `ci'` gets you inside the
first string; `j0f'` then `ci'` gets the error code on the next line.
In the JSON, `yyp` then `ci"` is the whole edit.

@solution
A route that measures 110 keystrokes.

**provider** — `/'SHIPPED'` `Enter` lands on the guard, because the error
code on the next line is `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED` and has no quote before
the `S`. Choosing a search that matches once is worth two seconds of
thought every time.

`4Y` takes the guard plus its trailing blank line. `3j` drops onto that
blank line and `p` puts the copy after it, which leaves both guards
spaced exactly like the original. Yanking the blank line with the block
is the trick: it means you never type a blank line or count one.

Then `f'` `ci'CANCELLED` `Esc`, `j0f'` `ci'ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED` `Esc`.
The `0` matters. `j` keeps the column, and after that first change the
column is well to the right of the quote you want, so `f'` would search
past it and miss.

**errors** — `:bn`, `/SHIPPED` `Enter`, `yyp`, `ci"ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED`
`Esc`. The status code and the sentry flag are already what you want, so
the copy needs one change, not three.

**resolver** — nothing. `:wqa` writes it back unchanged, which is not the
same as changing it.

The habit this lesson is drilling is duplicate-and-edit. In a codebase
with a house style, the thing you are about to write almost always
already exists four lines away. Copying it also copies the style, the
spacing and the surrounding punctuation, and every one of those is
somewhere a retyped version can differ.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    if (order.status === 'CANCELLED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

